<script>
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="`observe` — size, visibility and position as values">
        <p>An element's size, whether it is in view and where it sits are not values a field
            writes, so they are not binds. <code>observe</code> measures them, and
            <code>bind:element</code> takes what it hands back, one adaptor or a list of them.
            Each writes a reactive value you hand it, or calls a function you hand it instead.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it measures</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.size(box)}'}</code></td><td>the element's content box, as <code>{'{ width, height }'}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{"observe.size(box, { box: 'border' })"}</code></td><td>the border box instead, padding and border in</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.visible(seen)}'}</code></td><td>whether the element is in the viewport</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'observe.visible(seen, { in: scroller })'}</code></td><td>whether it is in view inside an element you hold, such as a scroller</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={observe.rect(placed)}'}</code></td><td>its rectangle in the viewport, as <code>getBoundingClientRect</code> reports it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'bind:element={[observe.rect(placed), observe.size(box)]}'}</code></td><td>both, on one element</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'return observe.size(refresh)(node)'}</code></td><td>the size, from inside a <code>bind:element</code> function of your own</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A measurement runs one way. The browser measures and writes the value, and nothing you
            write reaches the element. The first measurement lands after the first layout, so until
            then the value holds what you gave it. Every observed element shares one observer, and
            an element that leaves is no longer observed. The server has no layout and runs none of
            it.</p>

        <p>An adaptor is a function of the element that hands back what stops it, so a
            <code>bind:element</code> function of your own can call one on the node it is handed,
            beside the listeners it adds, and stop it with them. It shares the same one observer.</p>

        <p><code>in</code> takes an element, usually one <code>bind:element</code> gave you. Until
            it holds one, nothing is observed. The elements measured against one root share an
            observer, and that observer stops when the last of them leaves. <code>box</code> is
            <code>"content"</code> or <code>"border"</code>, and either box is written as
            <code>width</code> and <code>height</code> on screen, whatever the writing mode. Hand
            either option a reactive value and the element is measured again when it changes.</p>

        <p><code>observe.rect</code> reads again in the frame after the element resizes, the page or
            anything in it scrolls, the window resizes, or the element moves for any other reason,
            such as a sibling above growing: one read per element per frame, however many events
            arrived. Each observed element has an <code>IntersectionObserver</code> of its own, fitted
            to its last rectangle, which costs nothing while it stays put and is made again after
            each move. For a crosshair or a hover that follows the pointer, call
            <code>getBoundingClientRect()</code> in the event handler instead.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
